{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Kz3RBNz6eX2dXbntAP8pD","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have enough data to write a comprehensive architectural analysis. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Friction Report\n\nBased on systematic exploration of /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac, here are the concrete friction points ranked by leverage:\n\n### 1. **Parallel role-resolution wrappers (harden-core.sh:45-69, converge-core.sh:45-61)** — Duplication\nBoth loops wrap `almanac_loop_role_config` identically but with different role enums (harden: reviewer/conductor/fixer; converge: agent/overseer). The pattern—case-match roles, call shared resolver, provide field reader—is copy-pasted. **Friction:** Adding a 4th loop replicates the boilerplate; changing the shared role resolution API requires updates in two places.\n\n### 2. **Large multi-job function: `almanac_harden_fanout()` (harden-core.sh:980–1120, 140 lines)**\nOrchestrates target validation, lens loading, run registration, rubric immutability snapshot, worker fan-out, wait loop, and findings aggregation—all in one. Mixes orchestration logic (loop bounds, config validation) with worker health polling and ledger record ingestion. **Friction:** Hard to test in isolation; SIGPIPE invariant (line 67-68) buries a real concern in a wrapper; any change to fan-out protocol touches 140 lines.\n\n### 3. **Generic TSV record engine leaks loop-state-field knowledge (run.sh:41–44)**\n`almanac_loop_record_fields()` declares the canonical schema including `provider model effort iterations oversee lenses rounds queue_progress` — most are harden-only. The engine is generic, but the schema is loop-aware. **Friction:** Adding a converge-specific field (or vice versa) requires editing run.sh's central list, not the loop adapter; new loops must navigate a schema designed for harden's needs.\n\n### 4. **Inline HITL checkpoint with gum fallback (harden-core.sh:1464–1505, duplicated logic)**\nTTY detection + gum command invocation + plain fallback are repeated inline for both `choice` and `directive` prompts (lines 1470–1491). Two parallel if-then-gum-elif-read-else blocks. **Friction:** Operator semantics (TTY, gum detection) are scattered; a change to graceful-degradation behavior requires edits in multiple places.\n\n### 5. **Ledger record ingestion via multi-pass awk (harden-core.sh:598–662, 65 lines)**\n`almanac_harden_ledger_record()` reads findings, extracts existing statuses into a temp file, performs a three-file awk join (parsed/statuses/actions), then decides append/reopen. The complexity (4 temp files, 3 awk passes) is necessary but isolates the transition logic. **Friction:** Reopening logic (line 654–656) is buried inside, making the \"reopen on reproduction\" contract invisible to callers; testing the reopen decision without parsing findings is awkward.\n\n### 6. **Role case-match repeated per loop (harden-core.sh:49–52, converge-core.sh:48–50)**\nEach loop lists its own role enum (reviewer/conductor/fixer vs. agent/overseer) in a case statement. The check is lightweight but the pattern is duplicated. **Friction:** If a role-resolution contract ever needs central validation (e.g. \"all roles must implement X\"), it must be repeated per loop.\n\n### 7. **Worker orchestration dispatch implicit in agent.sh, explicit in run.sh (lib/agent.sh vs. lib/run.sh)**\n`lib/agent.sh` owns the agent_capture/agent_stream/agent_raw shapes and provider dispatch; `lib/run.sh` owns worker health and file paths but re-imports parts of the agent contract. **Friction:** Adding a new sandbox mode (beyond read-only/workspace-write/danger-full-access) requires coordinating between two modules.\n\n### 8. **Harden findings workflow assumes JSON-Lines reviewer output (harden-core.sh:387–437)**\nThe prompt (line 374–376) mandates JSON Lines; the parser (lines 387–437) has two implementations (jq-preferred, awk-fallback) but both assume the JSON schema. If converge ever needs different findings, the shared parser can't adapt. **Friction:** The reviewer output contract is baked into harden-core; a converge reviewer would need a separate output format, but the parsing boilerplate is shared.\n\n### 9. **Acceptance-met vs. open-blocking split across two functions (harden-core.sh:1375–1384, 1409–1410)**\nConvergence predicate checks acceptance (rubric checklist) + open blocking (ledger), but `almanac_harden_acceptance_met()` is a separate read of the rubric file and `almanac_harden_ledger_open_blocking()` is separate. The round verdict joins them but the reads are decoupled. **Friction:** If the gate logic ever changes (e.g. \"accept partial fixes\"), coordinating the two halves requires updates in multiple places.\n\n### 10. **Test coverage skew: harden has 2208 LOC tests, converge has 1293**\nHarden-core.sh (1989 LOC) is heavily tested; converge-core.sh (986 LOC) has lighter coverage. Converge's overseer parsing, role config, and dirty-path detection are all newer and less-exercised paths. **Friction:** Converge bugs are more likely to surface in production; adding a feature to converge may lack regression-test precedent.\n\n---\n\n**Top leverage fix:** Unify role-resolution (harden + converge) into a shared `almanac_loop_role_*` family in a new lib/role-harden.sh module, eliminating duplication and forcing new loops to use a consistent pattern. Ranks high because it surfaces loop-specific config at the seam level where it belongs."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19c2339b-02f8-4b81-9b44-59452ec22e33","uuid":"ebd8d96b-36b0-4bf4-81ba-be5c47438eaf","timestamp":"2026-05-26T18:16:13.184Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the almanac repo at /Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac to find architectural friction — shallow modules, leaky seams, modules where the interface is nearly as complex as the implementation, things hard to test through current interface.\n\nContext: this is a bash CLI for managing AI-agent loops. Key modules per CONTEXT.md:\n- lib/core.sh — CLI helpers + ALMANAC_HOME resolve\n- lib/ui.sh — gum-or-plain UI\n- lib/role.sh — role resolution\n- lib/agent.sh — agent_capture/agent_stream/agent_raw + provider dispatch\n- lib/providers/<name>.sh — provider adapters (auto-discovered)\n- lib/loops.sh — loop-adapter discovery + dispatch\n- lib/loops/<name>.sh — loop adapters (auto-discovered)\n- lib/feedback.sh — feedback detection + runner\n- lib/run.sh — run registry + run-status record + generic TSV record engine + control + hub views\n- lib/worker.sh — worker orchestration\n- lib/loop-launcher.sh — launcher\n- lib/harden-core.sh — harden loop\n- lib/converge-core.sh — converge loop\n\nThings I want you to inspect:\n1. lib/converge-core.sh and lib/harden-core.sh — look for parallel structures that should be unified or things that should be pushed up into shared infra\n2. lib/loops.sh dispatch — any leakage of loop-type knowledge outside adapters?\n3. lib/providers/*.sh — consistency of the contract across adapters\n4. lib/loop-launcher.sh — any per-loop branching that snuck in?\n5. cmd/*.sh — do they all use _die/_info from core.sh, or do some predate the rule?\n6. tests/ — what's well-tested vs hard-to-test\n7. Any function over ~100 lines doing multiple jobs\n8. Any duplicated patterns across loops/providers\n9. lib/run.sh — is the generic TSV record engine actually generic, or does it have loop-specific knowledge?\n\nReport what you find in concrete terms — file:line, the issue, why it's friction. Don't suggest fixes, just find friction. Aim for a punch list of 5-10 concrete issues, ranked by how much locality/leverage would improve if fixed.\n\nUnder 800 words.","agentId":"a4c3c0255433c142b","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now I have enough data to write a comprehensive architectural analysis. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Architectural Fricti